00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:07.710
how this is architected
and go through kind

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.320
of the day in the
life of a developer

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:13.800
as I push changes
out to production.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.540
But before we get
into that, I want

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.520
to step back just for a
second and sort of set

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.750
the stage a little bit and
kind of define serverless

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.045
as how we define it here.

00:03:22.045 --> 00:03:23.670
Serverless is one of
those words that's

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.225
kind of like DevOps
or CI itself where,

00:03:26.225 --> 00:03:28.350
depending on the context,
it means different things

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.251
to different people.

00:03:29.251 --> 00:03:31.000
The way that we're
going to use serverless

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.109
is really applications
and services

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:35.400
that you don't really have
to think too much about what

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.704
they're running on top of.

00:03:36.704 --> 00:03:39.120
So the idea is that you could
just bring your source code,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.453
and you don't have to think
about versioning or patching

00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:42.840
or provisioning or scaling.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.650
And all of that is
really done for you.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.150
The first thing
that typically comes

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:49.608
to mind when you
hear serverless is

00:03:49.608 --> 00:03:51.680
functions, and rightfully so.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.180
But we definitely see serverless
as a much broader ecosystem

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:55.170
than that.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.970
We think it includes
things like AI

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.190
and databases and
data warehousing.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.970
And on Google Cloud, we actually
offer serverless solutions

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.340
in each of those
different categories.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.950
When we talk about developer
solutions in CI/CD ,

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.660
we're really mainly focused
on serverless compute.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:12.660
And when we talk about
serverless compute,

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.760
there are really kind of
three primary solutions,

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.880
Google Cloud Functions,
which is relatively new,

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.579
Google App Engine, which
launched 10 years ago this year

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:24.330
as one of sort of the pioneers
when it comes to serverless,

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.120
and Firebase, which is kind
of part of the extended family

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.262
of the Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:29.262 --> 00:04:30.970
So that's sort of how
we're going to talk

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.590
about serverless itself.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:36.280
So when we talk about
CI/CD for serverless,

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.110
what do we mean exactly?

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:40.527
If you're running CI systems
now or you've built your own,

00:04:40.527 --> 00:04:42.193
you might recognize
some of these terms.

00:04:42.193 --> 00:04:44.380
You deal with things like
configuration management,

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:48.020
batch jobs, maybe ugly things
like nested virtualization

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.507
if you're trying to run
emulators for Android.

00:04:50.507 --> 00:04:52.840
Well, these are definitely
all the things that we do not

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:54.370
want to worry about anymore.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.210
We want all of these
problems to go away.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:57.280
I don't want to have
to think about having

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.470
to bake an immutable
image to deploy out

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.639
to my Cloud infrastructure.

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:03.430
The whole point of
serverless is to kind of

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:06.130
move these infrastructure
pieces below the waterline

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:08.710
so that your developers can
focus on source code and not

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:10.387
this underlying infrastructure.

00:05:10.387 --> 00:05:12.970
It would be a real shame if we
were able to move to serverless

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.160
and then our developers
had to provision a build

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.810
agent and configure it
to run their builds.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.240
So what do we mean for
CI/CD for serverless?

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.600
Well, it's all the
best pieces of CI/CD

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:28.140
in the first place that helps us
lower our mean time to release,

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:31.840
reduce human error,
deploy faster and safer.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.470
And for us, that primarily
revolves around automation.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:38.070
So as a developer, I want to sit
in my chair, drink my iced tea,

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:39.780
do a git push, and
then have everything

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.560
just sort of magically deploy
out to production for me.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.101
And along the way we're going
to have to do a couple of steps

00:05:45.101 --> 00:05:46.320
to our code to get it there.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.403
We might not be compiling
code, but we're probably

00:05:48.403 --> 00:05:51.150
going to be packaging up
with something like Webpack

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.770
or something like that.

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:55.186
We're definitely going to want
to automate all of our unit

00:05:55.186 --> 00:05:56.560
and integration tests.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.144
We all have sort
of 80%, 90% test

00:05:59.144 --> 00:06:00.810
coverage on our
applications, of course,

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:03.606
so we're going to want to make
sure we take advantage of that.

00:06:03.606 --> 00:06:05.980
We're also going to want to
have a manual review process.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:08.396
So if you're familiar with the
conversation between what's

00:06:08.396 --> 00:06:11.290
continuous delivery and
what's continuous deployment--

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.165
and the difference being
that there's usually

00:06:13.165 --> 00:06:14.580
a manual approval step--

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.610
in the real world,
almost every change

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.220
has to be reviewed by
somebody before it goes out.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.249
Whether it's a product owner
or management or design team,

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:25.290
we're going to want to
bake that into our process

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.050
that somebody can look at
and say OK, this looks good.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.384
This is what we had in mind.

00:06:30.384 --> 00:06:31.800
We're also going
to take advantage

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.174
of some of the best practices
around continuous delivery,

00:06:34.174 --> 00:06:37.320
so things like blue green
deployments and canary.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.630
And this is really to reduce
downtime to our users,

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:41.970
and also minimize
the blast radius

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.620
of any errors that do get out.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:45.411
We're not going to want
to deploy something

00:06:45.411 --> 00:06:46.740
to 100% of users right away.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.530
We're going to canary it out
gradually, sort of 5%, 10%

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:50.684
at a time.

00:06:50.684 --> 00:06:52.350
The last couple, as
we go through these,

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.130
we're going to want to
automate related tasks,

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:56.190
so things like creating
documentation and stuff

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.020
that we often tend to forget.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.980
And the last is consistent
build environments.

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:01.646
And this is just a
general best practice

00:07:01.646 --> 00:07:02.874
around developer tools.

00:07:02.874 --> 00:07:04.290
I think we all
know the phenomenon

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.900
of building locally and
then building in the Cloud

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.280
and not having
those two match up

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:09.488
and trying to figure out why.

00:07:12.244 --> 00:07:13.910
So if we look at this
kind of flowchart,

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:15.610
this probably looks
familiar to most of you.

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:17.818
This is sort of a classic
software delivery lifecycle

00:07:17.818 --> 00:07:18.620
chart.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:20.810
As a developer, I'm
going to do a git push.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:22.730
My source code is
then going to get

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:24.620
packaged and tested
and then deployed

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.000
to a staging environment.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.500
From there we're
going have a chance

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.500
to review and promote
that to production,

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.900
and ultimately deliver
it to our users.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:39.240
So when we talk about CI/CD,
it's more of a spectrum.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:41.870
It's more of a staircase than
it is like a light switch.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:46.790
We did a recent research asking
people, do you practice CI/CD?

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:48.710
And one of the questions
we asked, do you

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:51.350
practice true CI and true CD?

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:54.410
And only about 15% of
organizations said they did.

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.220
The vast majority were
self-described as well,

00:07:57.220 --> 00:07:59.190
we do kind of almost CI/CD.

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:02.160
We sort of do true CD, but
we sort of almost do CI.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.510
We kind of fake it
around the edges.

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:06.260
So what we find is
that most companies who

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:08.468
are trying to move towards
full end-to-end automation

00:08:08.468 --> 00:08:11.300
are still in that journey
and still getting there.

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:14.240
So if you look at the basic--
and this is in red because this

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.810
isn't really even
part of CI/CD--

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:19.690
but particularly in the
context of serverless,

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:21.710
we're tempted to do
manual deployments.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:25.027
If I'm changing a single file
or changing an HTML file,

00:08:25.027 --> 00:08:25.860
what's the big deal?

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.151
I can just run a deploy script.

00:08:27.151 --> 00:08:28.296
I can go to my terminal.

00:08:28.296 --> 00:08:30.170
Most of these have really
powerful CLI tools.

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:33.100
I can just manually deploy.

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:35.152
But pretty quickly
as a developer,

00:08:35.152 --> 00:08:36.110
I hit a wall with that.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.360
If I have any sort of team
that's developing together

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:41.340
on a source repo, if I
want to reduce errors,

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:44.277
I'm going to want to
sort of step up my game.

00:08:44.277 --> 00:08:46.610
And so particularly in the
context of CI/CD, what we see

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:49.120
is that a lot of the services,
more and more frequently,

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.290
are coming with baked in smarts.

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:53.780
So a lot of the best
practices, like things

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:57.020
like traffic splitting, that
we had to sort of create

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:00.500
ourselves and code, are
coming as baked in features

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:01.534
of the platform itself.

00:09:01.534 --> 00:09:03.200
And I think what we're
going to see over

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.602
time is that more and more
of these best practices

00:09:06.602 --> 00:09:08.810
come supported out of the
box, so that every time you

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:11.270
do a deployment, you can
provide the test script to run,

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:14.989
you can tell the branch of your
repo that you want to trigger.

00:09:14.989 --> 00:09:16.280
But we're not really there yet.

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:19.250
So I think as we said,
serverless tools are still

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:20.180
relatively new.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:21.389
It's still an evolving space.

00:09:21.389 --> 00:09:23.305
So this is definitely
where we're going to go,

00:09:23.305 --> 00:09:25.010
but in terms of
building a pipeline,

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:27.930
we can't really do it quite yet.

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:31.020
So the next step up is
what I kind of think

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.450
of as devcentric CI/CD.

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:35.730
And so in this world, it's
very developer-focused.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.815
As a developer, I want to
live in my source code.

00:09:37.815 --> 00:09:39.190
I want to live in
my code editor.

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:40.470
I want to live at the terminal.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:42.845
I want to make changes, and
then again, I want my changes

00:09:42.845 --> 00:09:44.970
to go out to production.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:46.710
Services in this
kind of category,

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:47.820
they're generally hosted.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:49.477
They're generally
pretty easy to use.

00:09:49.477 --> 00:09:52.060
And in this role, I really view
my repo as my source of truth.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:53.790
I want to be able
to go to my git repo

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:55.410
and get a pretty
good sense of what

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:59.910
users are seeing at each
stage of the environment.

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:03.390
And the final step on the ladder
is kind of opcentric CI/CD.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:05.610
So as we talk to customers,
particularly larger ones

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.950
in enterprises, the
devcentric model

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:09.670
tends to fall over
at some point.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:12.060
So larger customers tend to be--

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:14.400
they have very complex and
heterogeneous footprints,

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:15.900
and they're always
kind of migrating

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:17.100
between multiple services.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.410
So they may have some
sort of legacy platform

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:20.670
that's running on prem.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:23.580
They're probably deploying
to multiple clouds.

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:27.030
They may be deploying to
Kubernetes and VMs and Windows

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:28.590
and Linux and iOS.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:31.080
And operators who are
trying to put out fires

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:32.580
all the time, really,
they just want

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:35.160
like a single pane of
glass view across all

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.789
of these different deployments.

00:10:36.789 --> 00:10:38.830
And they also want to
enforce compliance as well.

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:40.250
They want to have
that bottleneck

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:43.140
so that they can enforce
security and compliance rules

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.270
across deployments everywhere.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:49.150
And in that category, if you're
familiar, worked with Google,

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:50.950
you may have heard
about Spinnaker.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.260
I know many of you here are
actually using it already.

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:55.990
Spinnaker is an open source,
multi-Cloud continuous delivery

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:57.100
platform.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.572
It's co-developed by
Google and Netflix.

00:10:59.572 --> 00:11:01.030
And we have a couple
of great talks

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:03.520
that really dive deeper
into that this week.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:04.990
Target is giving one.

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:06.580
Netflix is actually
here as well.

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:09.640
They're giving a talk with Waze
about how they use Spinnaker.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.210
But for the purpose
of this talk,

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:13.659
we're really going to focus
on the devcentric experience.

00:11:13.659 --> 00:11:15.450
And because we're
talking about serverless,

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:19.180
the lines between these
categories blur even more.

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:22.090
So we sort of talked about we
want to hide the operations

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:23.650
piece from our developers.

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:25.940
And serverless really
lets us do that.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.900
So we really want to achieve
full automation from the source

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:30.654
repo.

00:11:30.654 --> 00:11:32.320
So I think our first
takeaway, as I hope

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:33.280
we all sort of appreciate--

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:35.050
I imagine we're here in the
room because we generally

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:35.920
agree on this--

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.100
is that you absolutely still
need CI/CD solutions built

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:40.320
around this.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:44.800
And so at Google, this is what
we're excited to announce.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:46.970
So this week, and
earlier this morning,

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:49.090
you heard that we
announced Cloud Build.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.970
So Google Cloud Build is
a hosted CI/CD platform

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:54.880
running on Google Cloud for
automating your developer

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:56.050
workloads.

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:58.300
And this is actually an
evolution of another Google

00:11:58.300 --> 00:11:59.920
product, Container Builder.

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:02.800
So to give a brief history, if
you're familiar with Container

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:06.490
Builder, we launched this
a year ago at GCP Next 17

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:09.430
as a way to build docker
images quickly and easily.

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:12.610
You could point it at a
repo and do a git push,

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:15.220
and we would upload your source
code to a VM in the Cloud

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.800
that we managed, do a docker
build, and push that out

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.540
to container registry.

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:22.210
But from the very beginning,
the way we architected this,

00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:23.517
we sort of had grander visions.

00:12:23.517 --> 00:12:25.600
We thought, there's a lot
more we can do with this

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:26.725
than just build containers.

00:12:26.725 --> 00:12:29.110
We can really apply this
to not only containers,

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:32.730
but other workflows as well.

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:35.080
So let's take a look at of
how a workflow at Cloud Build

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:35.920
actually works.

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:39.370
And we can talk a little bit how
about it applies to serverless.

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:42.400
So we start as we always
do with source code.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:43.810
And in the case
of containers, we

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:46.950
end up with a built image
in the Container Registry.

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:50.050
And along the way, we upload
your source code to the Cloud,

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:53.590
to Google, and we put it
in a shared file system.

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:55.300
And against that
workspace, we're

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:56.920
going to run a series
of build steps.

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:59.214
So build steps are
the naming convention

00:12:59.214 --> 00:13:00.130
that Cloud Build uses.

00:13:00.130 --> 00:13:01.900
It's sort of like
stages or jobs,

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:04.726
if you're familiar with that
from other CI solutions.

00:13:04.726 --> 00:13:06.100
And in the context
of containers,

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:07.300
you may do things like this.

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:10.480
You may do a docker pull to
fetch a previously built image.

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:13.300
You may fetch some
dependencies, do a docker build,

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:17.150
and then push that
out to the registry.

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:19.949
But the key takeaway, or the
key way to look at Cloud Build

00:13:19.949 --> 00:13:22.490
and why it's different is that
these build steps are actually

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:24.239
just containers themselves.

00:13:24.239 --> 00:13:26.030
So everything that
happens in a Cloud Build

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:28.680
is a containerized task
that's completely isolated

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:31.480
and completely defined
by you as the user.

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:33.230
So because of that,
people pretty quickly,

00:13:33.230 --> 00:13:35.230
with Container Builder,
said hey, wait a minute,

00:13:35.230 --> 00:13:36.290
I can do a lot more.

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:38.826
I can run all sorts of different
things in the container.

00:13:38.826 --> 00:13:40.700
And so very quickly they
started doing things

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:42.570
like building other
types of artifacts.

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:45.290
So they were doing
gradle and maven builds

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:47.630
to build [INAUDIBLE] and
binaries, pushing that up

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:49.400
to Cloud storage.

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:50.690
They started running tests.

00:13:50.690 --> 00:13:53.120
You could run
arbitrary bash scripts,

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:56.620
you can do curl, things
like even Docker Compose.

00:13:56.620 --> 00:13:58.930
A Kubernetes user said,
well, I built the image

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:00.220
and I have it on my worker.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:01.595
Instead of pushing
to a registry,

00:14:01.595 --> 00:14:03.520
can I just kind of
do a git ops model?

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:06.880
Can I use kube CTL or Helm to
apply that out to the cluster?

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:09.467
And they started
using it that way.

00:14:09.467 --> 00:14:11.800
And I think what we also saw,
and the reason we're here,

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:16.120
is that it works really great
for serverless applications.

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:18.317
And that's because I think
one of the coolest parts

00:14:18.317 --> 00:14:20.650
about serverless is that
almost all of the best services

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:22.930
come with really
great CLI tools.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:25.720
So things like, sure, G
Cloud and Firebase, but even

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:29.950
throughout the ecosystem,
things like Netlify and Surge

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:31.180
and Web Task.

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:33.190
They all come with
really powerful CLIs

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:35.560
that really naturally
extend themselves

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:37.690
into containerized workloads.

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:40.340
And so what you can do is not
only build and test your code,

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:44.250
but also deploy it
out to serverless.

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:45.840
We call these things
builder images,

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:47.790
and these are the actual
containerized tasks

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:50.250
that you can use to run
your different workloads.

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:51.655
And these are all open source.

00:14:51.655 --> 00:14:53.280
And you can, of
course, bring your own.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:55.170
I think we have around
40 or 50 that are

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:56.850
contributed by the community.

00:14:56.850 --> 00:14:58.560
And people have done
all sorts of things.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.690
We have some for .NET.

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:02.110
There's an Android builder.

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:02.790
There's Packer.

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:04.260
There's Hugo.

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:06.060
You name it, and
just about anything

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:09.490
can be run as a container task.

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:12.620
So the second takeaway is that
not only do we really want

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:14.130
to still do CI/CD
for serverless,

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:16.640
but that you could use
Cloud Build as well

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:18.360
for that solution.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:21.210
And in fact, that's actually
exactly what we're going to do.

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:23.105
So let's switch back
over to our demo,

00:15:23.105 --> 00:15:24.980
and we're going to look
at the Prepster Store

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:29.480
here and see if we can't
go through a couple

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:33.110
real world examples of actually
making changes and then

00:15:33.110 --> 00:15:34.494
deploying this out.

00:15:34.494 --> 00:15:35.952
So if we could
switch to the demo .

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:39.270
Great.

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:40.950
Thank you.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:42.700
So let's look at our site here.

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:43.950
So this is our Prepster Shop.

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:47.350
This is live and up on the
web and ready for business.

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:49.750
And what we did is we
created two environments.

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:53.080
So first we created a
staging environment,

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:56.487
both on Firebase, which is here,
and a production environment.

00:15:56.487 --> 00:15:58.320
And all of our changes,
before hitting prod,

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:01.840
are going to pass through
our staging environment.

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:03.300
And if we look at
our source repo,

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:08.350
we'll see that we are using
sort of a small mono repo.

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:10.620
So obviously we could
use microservices.

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:12.630
But for the sake of
keeping things simple,

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:14.670
we decided to put
everything in one place,

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:16.240
so we have a back
end folder here,

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:19.980
which is an App Engine app,
front end running on Firebase,

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:23.250
and Cloud Functions.

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:26.704
So the site is looking
pretty good to me.

00:16:26.704 --> 00:16:27.870
But I got in on the morning.

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:29.670
I'm enjoying my
first cup of coffee.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:31.830
And the design team
comes running over,

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:34.980
and they say, hey, look,
the site looks really great.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.381
We really like this
header bar, but we really

00:16:37.381 --> 00:16:38.130
need to update it.

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:40.270
The red color, it's
looking a little stale.

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:41.940
We really want to
turn this to green.

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:43.620
So I said sure.

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:45.172
That is no problem.

00:16:45.172 --> 00:16:47.130
So I'm going to go back
here to my source code,

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:48.190
go into the front end.

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:51.190
This is an AngularJS app.

00:16:51.190 --> 00:16:54.970
Go into source, find the CSS
file where that's defined,

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:57.980
and make my change.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:16:59.400
So it's here, red.

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:00.480
Let's make it green.

00:17:03.432 --> 00:17:06.568
Commit that.

00:17:06.568 --> 00:17:07.859
So I'm going to commit that in.

00:17:07.859 --> 00:17:10.390
I'm now going to jump
over the Cloud Build UI.

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:12.875
So this is the Cloud
Build UI in Cloud Console.

00:17:12.875 --> 00:17:15.720
This is now a top level service,
so you'll find it directly

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:17.579
under Tools, and
it's no longer nested

00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:20.272
under Container Registry, if
you're familiar with that.

00:17:20.272 --> 00:17:21.980
And if I hit refresh,
I'll see that a new

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:23.050
build was kicked off.

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:28.079
So if I jump into that
build, we can take a look

00:17:28.079 --> 00:17:29.240
at what's happening.

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:31.740
So this build was kicked off
by my trigger, deploy front end

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:33.030
to staging.

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:34.650
It's in progress.

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:36.780
I'm using some encrypted
secrets for my Firebase

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:38.654
token for my ultimate
deployment to Firebase.

00:17:38.654 --> 00:17:41.590
And I'm going to run a
series of build steps here.

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:44.545
So first I'm going to install my
node modules using NPM install,

00:17:44.545 --> 00:17:46.170
and then I'm going
to do a simple build

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:47.444
using NPM run build.

00:17:47.444 --> 00:17:49.110
And then I'm just
going to deploy it out

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:52.350
with the regular Firebase
CLI doing Firebase deploy

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:56.350
to my staging environment.

00:17:56.350 --> 00:17:59.230
And if I look at the
trigger that set this up,

00:17:59.230 --> 00:17:59.980
I'll go over here.

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:01.010
So this is the trigger.

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:02.509
It's a Cloud Build
trigger, and this

00:18:02.509 --> 00:18:05.250
is what the logic that looks
for events on my repo and then

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:08.061
kicks off the pipeline
that I created.

00:18:08.061 --> 00:18:10.560
So for this one I'm going to
listen to changes on my staging

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:15.150
branch and only changes
that happen in my front end

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:15.750
directory.

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:18.384
So you saw that we set
it up as a mono repo.

00:18:18.384 --> 00:18:19.800
If I make a change
to my back end,

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:21.633
I obviously don't want
to rebuild and deploy

00:18:21.633 --> 00:18:22.200
my front end.

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:24.565
I only want to deploy
changes that are relevant.

00:18:24.565 --> 00:18:26.190
So this is a new
feature of Cloud Build

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:27.750
that's available as of today.

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:30.390
We call it file path triggering.

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:33.876
And it lets you filter triggers
basically on files that change.

00:18:33.876 --> 00:18:36.000
So we're going to look for
changes in our front end

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.750
folder, and we're actually going
to ignore some other files.

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:40.832
So if I change the
read me or git ignore,

00:18:40.832 --> 00:18:42.790
I don't want to trigger
a build on that either.

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:45.226
So we're going to ignore
any changes to those.

00:18:45.226 --> 00:18:46.600
And then when that
happens, we're

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:48.550
just going to use
our Cloud Build.YAML

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:52.410
to configure our build.

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:54.110
So if I go back over
here to the build,

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:59.250
check in on the progress,
and I fight the Wi-Fi,

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:02.520
we'll see that OK, the build
is successfully completed.

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:06.970
And if I go back to my staging
app and refresh, sure enough,

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:10.462
we see that my change was
successfully deployed.

00:19:10.462 --> 00:19:11.670
So I talk to the design team.

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:13.230
I say, does this look good?

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:15.330
They say, yep, looks great.

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:17.910
So when you go back
to my git repo here.

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:19.470
Looks like GitHub
was nice enough

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:23.240
to recommend the
pull request for me.

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:24.560
I'm going to create this.

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:27.110
And what I'm doing now to
promote it to production

00:19:27.110 --> 00:19:29.640
is simply merge it
into my master branch.

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:33.250
So I'm going to
merge this, confirm.

00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:34.170
Great.

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:36.060
So now this change
was on my master.

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:38.474
And I'm obviously
working from the UI

00:19:38.474 --> 00:19:40.890
here just so I don't have to
go through the full workflow.

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:42.790
But obviously,
from your terminal,

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:45.480
you can do a git commit and git
push to invoke this as well,

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:48.060
or use plug-ins with
your code editor.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:51.200
And if I go back to
Cloud Build, I'll

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:54.250
see another build
has been kicked off,

00:19:54.250 --> 00:19:56.624
which again, will do more
or less the same steps.

00:19:56.624 --> 00:19:58.790
But this time we're deploying
to production instead.

00:20:01.479 --> 00:20:03.020
So if we look at
the Cloud Build.YAML

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:06.770
to get a bit better sense
of exactly how we set this

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:10.610
up, let's go to front end, look
at the Cloud Build.YAML that

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:13.100
executed, we're going
to see more or less

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:15.420
the same build steps
that we saw before.

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:17.930
So this first one we're
installing our node modules.

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:20.492
This is the containerized
task that we're running.

00:20:20.492 --> 00:20:21.950
So we're going to
use NPM and we're

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:24.860
going to pass in our arguments
and just run NPM install.

00:20:24.860 --> 00:20:27.140
And we're going to do this
in the front end directory.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:29.480
We have a couple other steps
for linting and code testing

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:31.813
that we're just commenting
out for the sake of the demo,

00:20:31.813 --> 00:20:33.670
but we would probably
run these as well.

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:35.630
We're then going to
build with NPM run build

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:37.730
and then simply
deploy with Firebase.

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:41.430
And it comes with
built-in support for KMS,

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:42.950
which is key
management service, so

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:45.050
that you can encrypt your
tokens to talk to third party

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:45.550
services.

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:51.250
So if we go back to the
build, one other thing

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:52.980
that you might have noticed,
if you're using Container Build

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.090
or that you haven't
seen yet, is view

00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:58.010
this build in the new
Build Result interface.

00:20:58.010 --> 00:21:06.170
So if we click on that
and fight the Wi-Fi--

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:16.280
whoa.

00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:17.300
That is not a feature.

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:21.660
The demo gods were kind.

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:23.050
Here we go.

00:21:23.050 --> 00:21:24.959
So this is our new
build result UI.

00:21:24.959 --> 00:21:27.000
And you can see that we're
running the same build

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:28.025
that we ran before.

00:21:28.025 --> 00:21:30.150
So we have steps where we
install our node modules,

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:32.580
run our builds, and deploy.

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:34.494
And this is currently
in private alpha,

00:21:34.494 --> 00:21:36.660
but this is going to be
rolling out later this year.

00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:38.409
And what we heard from
users over and over

00:21:38.409 --> 00:21:41.105
was that the UI is really
great, but as a developer,

00:21:41.105 --> 00:21:43.230
I really want to be able
to debug my builds quickly

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:44.880
and also have more
of a dashboard

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:47.460
view of what the status of my
different deployed applications

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:48.480
are.

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:54.260
So we're building a new UI from
scratch to help enable that.

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:56.020
So if I look here,
it says that looks

00:21:56.020 --> 00:21:58.100
like this build was successful.

00:21:58.100 --> 00:22:03.320
And if I go back to production
here and refresh, sure enough,

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:05.299
my change is out and I'm done.

00:22:05.299 --> 00:22:07.590
So I send a Slack message to
the design team that says,

00:22:07.590 --> 00:22:10.887
good news, our changes are live.

00:22:10.887 --> 00:22:11.720
So I'm feeling good.

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:13.280
I'm going back to my coffee.

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:16.784
And before I can relax too much,
I see the sales team runs over.

00:22:16.784 --> 00:22:18.200
The sales team
runs over and says,

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:20.805
hey, Christopher,
good news, bad news.

00:22:20.805 --> 00:22:22.180
The good news is
the linen blazer

00:22:22.180 --> 00:22:24.260
has been extremely popular.

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:26.980
The bad news is that we're sold
out, so we don't have any left.

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:30.140
And can we update the
database to indicate that

00:22:30.140 --> 00:22:33.260
so that we don't get any more
orders before we get more in?

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:35.900
So we say sure, no problem.

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:38.000
So for that we're going
to jump into our back end.

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:40.730
So if we go back
to our repo here,

00:22:40.730 --> 00:22:42.620
this is just a regular
App Engine app.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:44.390
We've created two
versions of it,

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:47.180
one for staging, which we're
going to deploy to our staging

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:49.610
service, and one to
production, which we're just

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:53.230
going to use our default. And
if we jump into our App JS

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:58.230
and find out where our API
response is coming from--

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:02.540
this is our fancy inventory
management service here--

00:23:02.540 --> 00:23:05.840
turn this to true.

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:08.160
Commit to staging.

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:09.490
There we go.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:14.280
And if we go back to Cloud
Build again, refresh,

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:17.880
we'll see that a new
build has been kicked off.

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:20.010
This one is triggering on
deploy back into staging,

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:23.092
so again, we're looking only for
changes to our back end folder.

00:23:23.092 --> 00:23:24.300
And this one's pretty simple.

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:27.120
We're just running basically
a gcloud app deploy

00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:29.304
to our staging service.

00:23:29.304 --> 00:23:31.470
One thing that's interesting
that you'll notice here

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:34.110
is that we're actually going
to pass in a version ID.

00:23:34.110 --> 00:23:36.120
So if you're familiar
with App Engine,

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:37.830
if you don't specify
one, App Engine

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:39.814
will provide you
with a unique ID.

00:23:39.814 --> 00:23:41.730
But it doesn't really
tell you much about what

00:23:41.730 --> 00:23:42.990
was actually deployed.

00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:46.020
So we're actually going to pass
in the commit SHA as our ID

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:47.807
to all of our App
Engine deployments.

00:23:47.807 --> 00:23:49.890
And this way, if we go to
our App Engine interface

00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:51.870
and want to understand
what's in production

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:54.390
or what's on staging, we can
really quickly and easily map

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:56.231
it back to the commit
SHA so we know exactly

00:23:56.231 --> 00:23:57.480
what source code was in there.

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:03.540
So if we let that change
go out, looks like the

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:07.610
build is completing,
and it's done.

00:24:07.610 --> 00:24:12.450
I will go over here,
refresh, and let's see.

00:24:12.450 --> 00:24:15.600
App Engine can be fairly
efficient with its caching

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:16.170
sometimes.

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:19.320
So if I go to the
API response and give

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:22.850
that a chance to refresh here.

00:24:22.850 --> 00:24:24.750
And OK, so sold out is true.

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:27.440
So we should see that as
well on our front end now.

00:24:27.440 --> 00:24:28.970
And we do.

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:32.410
So our linen blazer here
is transferred to sold out

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:35.652
and I can no longer buy it.

00:24:35.652 --> 00:24:36.360
Looks good to me.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:37.980
It's a pretty
straightforward change.

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:40.130
So let's do the same
trick we did before.

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:42.680
Let's go back to our code repo.

00:24:42.680 --> 00:24:51.720
Let's merge this into
master and push that out.

00:24:54.664 --> 00:24:56.080
And so for our
push to production,

00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:58.090
we're actually going to do a
little something different.

00:24:58.090 --> 00:25:00.790
So we talked earlier about how
we want to canary changes out

00:25:00.790 --> 00:25:03.820
to users and not necessarily
deploy them to 100% of people

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:05.140
all at once.

00:25:05.140 --> 00:25:08.730
And if we go in here
to our build and we

00:25:08.730 --> 00:25:11.400
look at the build
steps that ran,

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:13.060
we're doing some
more things here.

00:25:13.060 --> 00:25:14.610
So let's look at
our Cloud Build.YAML

00:25:14.610 --> 00:25:20.120
and investigate a little
further out what's going on.

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:21.550
So we're going to
go to our Cloud

00:25:21.550 --> 00:25:23.120
Build.YAML for production.

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:26.170
And what we're going to do
is actually-- so the way

00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:29.500
App Engine works is that
when you do a deployment

00:25:29.500 --> 00:25:30.970
and you give it
your source code,

00:25:30.970 --> 00:25:33.280
it actually builds a
container under the hood

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:35.500
using Cloud Build
itself, and then deploys

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:36.347
that container out.

00:25:36.347 --> 00:25:37.930
So when we're deploying
in production,

00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:40.600
we're actually going to do
this process ourself manually.

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:42.010
Instead of deploying
source code,

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:44.010
we're going to build the
container ahead of time

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:45.140
and then pass that in.

00:25:45.140 --> 00:25:47.140
So this way I actually
now have an artifact

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:48.806
of what was released
at any time.

00:25:48.806 --> 00:25:50.680
So if anything ever goes
wrong in production,

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:53.030
I could pull that container
down and run it locally.

00:25:53.030 --> 00:25:55.690
I can also use it for things
like integration tests

00:25:55.690 --> 00:25:57.920
and smoke tests and
things like that.

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:01.780
So we're going to do NPM test,
we're going to do docker build,

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:05.050
and then we're going to use the
image URL flag to deploy it out

00:26:05.050 --> 00:26:06.647
to App Engine.

00:26:06.647 --> 00:26:08.230
The other thing that
we're going to do

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:10.630
is we're actually going
to canary this change out.

00:26:10.630 --> 00:26:12.460
So we're going to use
the no promote flag

00:26:12.460 --> 00:26:15.280
to deploy the version but not
actually send any traffic over

00:26:15.280 --> 00:26:15.814
yet.

00:26:15.814 --> 00:26:17.230
And what we're
going to do here is

00:26:17.230 --> 00:26:20.722
we're going to actually do
a little of scripting here.

00:26:20.722 --> 00:26:22.180
So everything we've
done so far has

00:26:22.180 --> 00:26:24.940
been pretty straightforward,
like Firebase deploy, G Cloud

00:26:24.940 --> 00:26:26.084
App deploy.

00:26:26.084 --> 00:26:28.000
For canary now, we're
going to jump into Bash.

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:28.958
So this is a container.

00:26:28.958 --> 00:26:31.670
I can just set my entry
point to anything I want.

00:26:31.670 --> 00:26:34.570
And we're going to grab
the version that users

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:35.650
are currently seeing.

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:37.780
So we're going to do G
Cloud App versions list,

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:39.905
sort it by the amount of
traffic that it's getting,

00:26:39.905 --> 00:26:41.370
and just grab the first one.

00:26:41.370 --> 00:26:43.720
We're then going to have a
really very pretty Python

00:26:43.720 --> 00:26:46.240
script to grab that
version ID and then

00:26:46.240 --> 00:26:49.330
use the Cloud set traffic
field to programmatically

00:26:49.330 --> 00:26:50.910
canary this change out.

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:51.700
And what we're
going to do is we're

00:26:51.700 --> 00:26:53.283
going to take the
version that we just

00:26:53.283 --> 00:26:55.999
deployed, which we tagged to the
commit SHA, and give that 5%.

00:26:55.999 --> 00:26:57.790
We're then going to
take the version that's

00:26:57.790 --> 00:27:02.040
currently getting traffic
and give that 95%.

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:05.030
And so if we go over to App
Engine here and hit refresh,

00:27:05.030 --> 00:27:06.860
sure enough, we'll
see that the build

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:09.050
that we just did is
currently being rolled out

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:11.682
to 5% of our users.

00:27:11.682 --> 00:27:13.640
And what's great is that
App Engine comes baked

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:15.720
in with things like
traffic splitting.

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:19.010
So we can go in here, and then
assuming things look good,

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:20.544
roll this out as we see fit.

00:27:20.544 --> 00:27:22.335
So we're just going to
roll it out to 100%.

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:27.550
So that was saved successfully.

00:27:27.550 --> 00:27:30.130
And if we go back, we'll see
that we have successfully

00:27:30.130 --> 00:27:31.180
canaried this change out.

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:39.950
So let's just confirm
here that actually worked.

00:27:39.950 --> 00:27:40.450
Yes.

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:42.324
And sure enough, it's
in production and live.

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:45.300
All right.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:47.675
So if we take a little breath
and just look at everything

00:27:47.675 --> 00:27:50.880
we just accomplished, with just
a couple of changes to our git

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:53.970
repo, we're able to
stage and deploy changes

00:27:53.970 --> 00:27:56.195
to both our front
end and our back end.

00:27:56.195 --> 00:27:58.320
And if you notice, we were
jumping around in the UI

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:00.870
to show you what was going
on behind the scenes.

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:03.000
But really, as a
developer, I never

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:04.959
really had to leave
my code editor.

00:28:04.959 --> 00:28:06.750
I could just do all
this from the terminal.

00:28:06.750 --> 00:28:09.614
I could do all this from
GitHub or the source repository

00:28:09.614 --> 00:28:12.030
that you're using, and just
push those changes out and not

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:14.580
have to worry about anything.

00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:16.724
So we're feeling pretty good.

00:28:16.724 --> 00:28:17.890
I was going to take a break.

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:20.580
But I see the CEO
is running over.

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:24.250
The CEO comes over, and we had
previously set up an alert.

00:28:24.250 --> 00:28:26.550
So every time we made a
large sale, as a startup,

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:27.970
we're very excited.

00:28:27.970 --> 00:28:30.090
We set it up so that
she gets a text message,

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:32.514
so that she got this
gong in the office,

00:28:32.514 --> 00:28:33.930
and every time we
have a big sale,

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:36.660
she likes to go and ring
the gong and celebrate.

00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:38.940
Turns out she got a new
phone number over the weekend

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:41.460
and really needs us
to update where we're

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:43.835
sending those text messages to.

00:28:43.835 --> 00:28:45.960
So for that, we're going
to jump back into our code

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:49.047
and go into Cloud Functions.

00:28:49.047 --> 00:28:51.130
So Cloud Functions is
definitely one of the things

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:54.250
that we hear from
customers the most about,

00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:57.760
how do I run functions or
manage functions at scale?

00:28:57.760 --> 00:29:00.190
Like when I have one
function or two functions,

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:03.490
it's pretty straightforward, but
if I start getting 50 or 100,

00:29:03.490 --> 00:29:05.740
how can I automate
this in a safe way?

00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:08.740
I don't want to have to
manually update my index.js file

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:10.390
every time I create
a new one, and I

00:29:10.390 --> 00:29:12.700
don't want to deploy all my
functions every single time

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:14.270
I make a change.

00:29:14.270 --> 00:29:17.039
So we're going to do a couple
of things via convention

00:29:17.039 --> 00:29:18.580
and a couple of
things via technology

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:20.050
here to make sure
that this works.

00:29:20.050 --> 00:29:21.550
So the first thing
we're going to do

00:29:21.550 --> 00:29:23.758
is we're going to put our
functions in this functions

00:29:23.758 --> 00:29:24.579
folder here.

00:29:24.579 --> 00:29:25.870
And so these are our functions.

00:29:25.870 --> 00:29:28.127
On large sale is the
one we care about.

00:29:28.127 --> 00:29:29.210
But we have a whole bunch.

00:29:29.210 --> 00:29:34.990
So we have auth log in log
out, user on create on delete.

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:38.350
And if you notice that we added
an extension here to each one

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:41.530
of these .f.js, and this is
going to let us do some kind

00:29:41.530 --> 00:29:44.680
of fancy stuff.

00:29:44.680 --> 00:29:46.251
So if we go to
our index.js file,

00:29:46.251 --> 00:29:48.000
we're going to do a
couple of tricks here.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:51.180
Some you may be familiar
with, some may be new.

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:52.890
So the way functions
works is that it

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:56.346
looks for exported functions
in your index.js file.

00:29:56.346 --> 00:29:57.720
And we're going
to do is actually

00:29:57.720 --> 00:30:00.600
glob over that functions
folder, grab every file that

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:03.000
ends in f.js, and
then we're going

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:07.030
to loop over it and convert that
file path to a function name.

00:30:07.030 --> 00:30:08.409
So in the case of
auth on create,

00:30:08.409 --> 00:30:10.200
we're going to convert
that to a camel case

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:13.170
to a function name
called auth on create.

00:30:13.170 --> 00:30:14.850
And then we're actually
going to create

00:30:14.850 --> 00:30:16.710
two versions of every function.

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:18.210
So for every function
that we have,

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:20.790
we're going to create
one regular version, auth

00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:22.604
on create, and one for staging.

00:30:22.604 --> 00:30:24.520
So we're going to append
an underscore staging

00:30:24.520 --> 00:30:26.580
to every single function
as well and make

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:30.300
both of these
available to ourselves.

00:30:30.300 --> 00:30:33.480
So we go back to our code here.

00:30:33.480 --> 00:30:37.120
And let's go ahead and make that
change and see what happens.

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:40.920
So I go in here, and we're using
Twilio to send an SMS message.

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:44.880
And I'm going to
update the phone number

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:45.870
and commit that out.

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:54.610
And if I go over to
Cloud Build, hit refresh,

00:30:54.610 --> 00:30:56.440
there's our new build.

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:58.361
We're going to jump in.

00:30:58.361 --> 00:30:59.777
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:30:59.777 --> 00:31:03.656
going to do this function
called deploy updated functions.

00:31:03.656 --> 00:31:06.280
So let's look at the Cloud Build
and see what's happening here.

00:31:12.416 --> 00:31:14.290
So you see again, we're
doing the same steps.

00:31:14.290 --> 00:31:17.020
We're going to use yarn this
time to do yarn install.

00:31:17.020 --> 00:31:19.789
We have some unit tests
that we've commented out.

00:31:19.789 --> 00:31:21.580
So what we're going to
do is we're actually

00:31:21.580 --> 00:31:25.090
going to create a cached
version of all the functions

00:31:25.090 --> 00:31:27.850
that we deploy and put
it up in Cloud storage.

00:31:27.850 --> 00:31:29.510
And then every time
we do a deployment,

00:31:29.510 --> 00:31:32.030
we're going to recursively loop
over that functions folder,

00:31:32.030 --> 00:31:36.749
look for changes, and deploy
only those files that changed.

00:31:36.749 --> 00:31:38.540
So we could walk through
that a little bit.

00:31:38.540 --> 00:31:40.270
So the first step is
we're going to grab

00:31:40.270 --> 00:31:42.790
our functions.staging
folder from the Cloud

00:31:42.790 --> 00:31:46.000
and look at what functions
are currently live in staging.

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:47.620
We're then going to
do a diff and loop

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:51.340
over comparing our local
function changes to the changes

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:52.750
that are out in staging.

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:55.480
We're going to use this .n flag
to look for new files as well

00:31:55.480 --> 00:31:56.800
that we've introduced.

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:59.620
And then for each
file that's different,

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:02.350
we're going to convert that
file path to a name again using

00:32:02.350 --> 00:32:05.140
our camel case convention, and
then just deploy that function

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:07.594
out, this time to staging.

00:32:07.594 --> 00:32:09.760
And we're going to pass in
our environment variables

00:32:09.760 --> 00:32:13.620
using KMS again for Twilio.

00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:20.470
So if we look at our build,
see how that's coming along,

00:32:20.470 --> 00:32:22.480
then we can go on here,
we can look at logs.

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:25.170
We can reverse these.

00:32:25.170 --> 00:32:27.540
So looks like it's deployed.

00:32:27.540 --> 00:32:29.410
Looks like it just finished.

00:32:29.410 --> 00:32:31.554
And if we go to Cloud
Functions, so this

00:32:31.554 --> 00:32:33.970
is what it looks like when
I've deployed all my functions.

00:32:33.970 --> 00:32:37.260
So for every function that we
had, authOnLogin, authLogout,

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:39.810
we have two versions,
staging and prod.

00:32:39.810 --> 00:32:43.990
And if we hit refresh, we'll
notice that of the ones

00:32:43.990 --> 00:32:47.880
that these deployed, only this
one, on large sale staging,

00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:49.399
was updated recently.

00:32:49.399 --> 00:32:51.690
So we didn't have to deploy
any of the other functions.

00:32:51.690 --> 00:32:53.490
And as you get to scale
with hundreds of functions.

00:32:53.490 --> 00:32:54.840
This becomes really nice.

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:56.756
So we just deployed the
only one that changed.

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:00.210
And if we liked how that went.

00:33:00.210 --> 00:33:03.060
We can go back and
again, do the same thing,

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:06.090
merge that into master and
then deploy that out as well.

00:33:13.757 --> 00:33:15.840
So now this is just going
to go ahead and roll out

00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:17.220
to production.

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:20.400
And our CEO will successfully
get all text message updates

00:33:20.400 --> 00:33:24.480
and be able to celebrate
our wins with our team.

00:33:27.910 --> 00:33:28.670
Great.

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:30.290
So that's more or less our demo.

00:33:30.290 --> 00:33:32.270
As a developer, I
was able to pretty

00:33:32.270 --> 00:33:35.630
quickly and easily, in, about 10
or 20 minutes push changes out

00:33:35.630 --> 00:33:39.530
to Firebase App Engine and
Cloud Functions with little

00:33:39.530 --> 00:33:41.090
to no effort outside
of just doing

00:33:41.090 --> 00:33:43.380
a simple git to push deploy.

00:33:43.380 --> 00:33:47.010
So if we go back to
the presentation,

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:49.100
this was all done
with Cloud Build.

00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:50.859
And so with Cloud
Build, just real quickly

00:33:50.859 --> 00:33:53.150
we're going to go through
some of the new features that

00:33:53.150 --> 00:33:55.720
are available today that
we used in the demo.

00:33:55.720 --> 00:33:57.260
It's top level in
the Cloud console,

00:33:57.260 --> 00:33:58.700
so you'll see it there.

00:33:58.700 --> 00:34:01.460
We're adding support for
first class artifacts.

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:03.380
So the same way that
you could push images

00:34:03.380 --> 00:34:05.990
to a Container Registry,
you can now push artifacts

00:34:05.990 --> 00:34:06.930
to Cloud storage.

00:34:06.930 --> 00:34:08.989
So if we're building
things like binaries

00:34:08.989 --> 00:34:11.434
or just regular folders.

00:34:11.434 --> 00:34:13.100
We added support for
file path triggers,

00:34:13.100 --> 00:34:15.469
which we used pretty
extensively in order to filter

00:34:15.469 --> 00:34:17.469
based on sub directory.

00:34:17.469 --> 00:34:19.010
And then we have
two new changes that

00:34:19.010 --> 00:34:20.719
are currently in
private alpha but are

00:34:20.719 --> 00:34:22.146
going to be coming soon.

00:34:22.146 --> 00:34:24.770
This one is probably the one I'm
most excited about and the one

00:34:24.770 --> 00:34:26.311
that we hear from
customers the most,

00:34:26.311 --> 00:34:29.060
is a deeper integration
with GitHub itself.

00:34:29.060 --> 00:34:33.199
So we're adding native built-in
support for pull requests,

00:34:33.199 --> 00:34:34.940
posting status back
to the new checks

00:34:34.940 --> 00:34:37.610
API, as well as the ability
to programmatically create

00:34:37.610 --> 00:34:39.230
triggers via the API.

00:34:39.230 --> 00:34:40.730
So instead of having
to go to the UI

00:34:40.730 --> 00:34:42.604
if you're creating lots
and lots of triggers,

00:34:42.604 --> 00:34:44.116
you can do it now via the API.

00:34:44.116 --> 00:34:45.949
And that's all going
to be rolling out soon,

00:34:45.949 --> 00:34:47.570
is available in alpha.

00:34:47.570 --> 00:34:51.560
And then last is the new
result UI that we showed.

00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:53.670
So why Cloud Build
for serverless?

00:34:53.670 --> 00:34:55.467
It's serverless, too, kind of.

00:34:55.467 --> 00:34:57.300
We didn't really have
to provision anything.

00:34:57.300 --> 00:34:59.000
We didn't really have to think
about what it was running on.

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:01.230
We just submitted a
Cloud Build.YAML file.

00:35:01.230 --> 00:35:02.647
It's pay as you go.

00:35:02.647 --> 00:35:03.855
It's a pretty good free tier.

00:35:03.855 --> 00:35:06.050
It's 120 free minutes a day.

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:07.792
The builds, particularly
for serverless,

00:35:07.792 --> 00:35:09.000
were one or two minutes long.

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:11.640
So this is a pretty good deal.

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:12.725
It's highly configurable.

00:35:12.725 --> 00:35:14.100
You can bring your
own container,

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:16.204
you can run your own tasks.

00:35:16.204 --> 00:35:17.370
And it's developer friendly.

00:35:17.370 --> 00:35:19.400
So everything we showed
was running in the Cloud.

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:21.450
But it actually comes with
the local binary, Cloud Build

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:23.730
Local, so you can run
builds on your workstation

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:28.380
and you can trigger
builds from API or SDK.

00:35:28.380 --> 00:35:30.780
So if we go back to
our flow diagram here,

00:35:30.780 --> 00:35:33.780
I think we showed doing a
git pushed through staging.

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:35.760
And we were able to use
Cloud Build in order

00:35:35.760 --> 00:35:37.800
to improve your
developer velocity

00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:40.670
and increase faster and safer
deployments to serverless run

00:35:40.670 --> 00:35:44.340
times like App Engine, Cloud
Functions, and Firebase.

00:35:44.340 --> 00:35:45.330
Thank you very much.

00:35:45.330 --> 00:35:50.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

